<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Committee of the Red Cross</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as ICRC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Committee of the Red Cross is a humanitarian organisation founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1863 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-dunant">Henry Dunant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillaume-henri-dufour">Guillaume-Henri Dufour</a> and three other citizens. It inspired the Geneva Conventions and works to protect and assist victims of war, visiting prisoners of war, tracing missing persons and promoting humanitarian law. It received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1917, 1944 and 1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-committee-of-the-red-cross.jpg" alt="International Committee of the Red Cross" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: International Committee of the Red Cross · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_ICRC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Until the middle of the 19th century armies had no organised nursing systems for their casualties and no safe institutions in which to treat the wounded. In June 1859 the Swiss businessman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-dunant">Henry Dunant</a> reached Solferino on the evening of 24 June and saw the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Battle of Solferino</a>, in which about 40,000 soldiers died or were left wounded in a single day. Shocked by the suffering and the near-total lack of medical care, he spent several days helping to treat the wounded, organising local people to give aid without discrimination.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> he wrote A Memory of Solferino, published at his own expense in 1862 and sent to leading figures across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It called for national voluntary relief organisations to nurse wounded soldiers and for international treaties guaranteeing the neutrality and protection of the wounded and of medics. The first Geneva Convention was signed in 1864 by 16 countries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 15 October 1914, soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, the Committee set up an International Prisoners-of-War Agency. By the war's end the Agency had handled about 20 million letters and messages and 1.9 million parcels, and its intervention led to the exchange of about 200,000 prisoners. Its card index grew to about 7 million records and led to the identification of about 2 million prisoners. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a> of 1932 the Committee visited 18,000 Bolivian prisoners and 2,500 Paraguayan detainees.</p><p>In 1995 its president, Cornelio Sommaruga, took part in the ceremony marking the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> and publicly regretted the past mistakes and shortcomings of the Red Cross towards the victims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its operations rest on international humanitarian law: the four Geneva Conventions of 1949, the Additional Protocols of 1977 and 2005, and its own statutes. Its tasks include monitoring compliance with the Conventions, supervising the treatment of prisoners of war, tracing missing persons and acting as a neutral intermediary between warring parties. It prefers low-key confidential negotiation to public pressure, reasoning that this gains greater access; in apartheid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> it was granted access to prisoners such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, who later praised it. Its motto is Inter Arma Caritas, amidst war, charity.</p><p>The Assembly, of at most twenty-five Swiss citizens, sets policy, and the Directorate manages daily affairs. The Committee had only twelve employees in 1914. In 2005 it adopted the Red Crystal as a protective symbol, and Magen David Adom, which uses the Star of David, was accepted into the Movement in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “International Committee of the Red Cross” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geneva became the centre of international humanitarian law after Henry Dunant and four fellow citizens founded the <b>International Committee of the Red Cross</b> in 1863, and the first Geneva Convention was signed there in 1864.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It became the <b>International Committee of the Red Cross</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
